we're going to start our third panel  with a paper by Jeff Richmond mole who  is a doctoral student in the department  of art history of the University of  Delaware study in nineteenth and early  twentieth century American art he's a  recent recipient of the elva melon  bruise pre-doctoral fellowship for  historians of American Art to travel  abroad from the Center for Advanced  Study individual arts at the national  gallery of art in Washington DC he  graduated from Princeton University with  an a B in our history and received a  graduate certificate in Christian  Studies from Westminster Theological  Seminary in Philadelphia he has worked  in the curatorial departments of  numerous museums including the New York  Historical Society the Pennsylvania  Academy of the Fine Arts and the  Princeton University Art Museum in 2014  he organized the exhibition blessed are  the peacemakers violet oakleys the angel  of victory at the Delaware art museum  which forms the basis of his talk today  well thank you so much it's a pleasure  and privilege to to be here today and  thanks to the conference organizers and  to to yell into the other presenters  it's been really fascinating conference  so far so it's all very exciting so I'm  get started despite its completion just  two weeks after the Japanese attack on  Pearl Harbor in the United States entry  into World War two violet oakley is the  angel victory nevertheless announces  peace and victory to its viewer war and  peace may indeed appear paradoxical at  this volatile point in American and  world history but this paper will show  how Oakley negotiated the violence of  this moment by infusing her largely  unstudied wartime work  with her lifelong vision for a just and  peaceful world the Archangel Michael at  left in st. George at right stand with  swords sheathed having already defeated  their evil reptilian foes while the  angel Gabriel extends a palm of victory  and peace at center with the victory  against evil already guaranteed here  Oakley declares America's own fight to  be unusable thereby providing constantly  embattled troops with a hope comfort and  confidence that peace one day would come  and the certainty and sanctity of  American victory in oakleys altarpiece  echoes I think President Franklin Delano  Roosevelt's words to Congress just one  day after Pearl Harbor Roosevelt  declared no matter how long it may take  us to overcome this premeditated  invasion the American people in their  righteous might will win through to  absolute victory with confidence in our  armed forces with the unbounding  determination of our people we will gain  the inevitable triumph so help us God  the angel of victory provided a  prophetic vision and perhaps even a  scriptural grounding for Roosevelt's and  the nation's faith in a future American  triumph as FDR's declaration suggests  then the paintings epic apocalyptic  language is not idiosyncratic instead it  evokes broader American sentiments  regarding war itself in the role of art  and times of military conflict timely  questions given our many recent  opportunities to reflect on imagery and  more time from the centennial of World  War one to contemporary conflicts abroad  it's perhaps most fitting that on this  70th anniversary of the Allied victory  in Europe we should examine oakleys  triptych this is a proleptic picture of  certain triumphs painted at the advent  of war and intended for a naval base  that became a major embarkation point  for American troops headed to Europe and  short oakleys painting is a pre-emptive  VE Day proclamation in addition to the  fact that oakleys career as a whole and  her wartime works in particular have  received little attention among  historians her altar pieces are helpful  in correcting the current critical  understanding of religious art in the  Second World War in his 2014 book the  great and holy war  Philip Jenkins argues that unlike the  essentially Christian nature of the  first world war world war two was quote  starkly different in its religious  character for Jenkins and quote again  the second world war produced no overtly  religious manifestations vaguely vaguely  comparable with those of the first  making it very difficult to find  propaganda imagery depicting  supernatural intervention on the  battlefield of the kind that was so  commonplace a generation earlier and  here are works from that world war one  generation in short Jenkins Jenkins  writes angels steered clear of this  conflict so what then of the angel  victory what of the many other  altarpieces painted for American  Chaplin's throughout World War two these  works begin to suggest how this war was  in fact represented and understood in  supernatural terms that visions and  apparitions did indeed visit troops  stationed at home and on the Battlefront  despite Jenkins and others assertions to  the contrary in addition then to  reconciling the peace oakley envisioned  in December 1941 with the conflict to  come I hope to account for the religious  character of her efforts and an era when  as Jenkins and other writers have argued  public religion was otherwise believed  to have been racked beyond recovery an  illustrator portraitist and the first  American woman to find fame in the  burgeoning field of public mural  painting oakley devoted her 60-year  career to a quest for peace inspired by  the Quaker faith she adopted the slogan  that pacifists are not passive s but  peacemakers oakley thus considered her  art to be a vehicle for social change  believing that beauty can lift up  communities and bring about an ethical  society and so in a speech entitled the  place of art and an embattled world  given at the contemporary Club in New  York City in March 1940 Oakley described  the dream in which she spoke with Adolf  Hitler and appealing to his years as an  amateur artist attempted to persuade the  Nazi leader on these terms quote I seem  to be urging him to return to his  abandoned art to seek to express himself  in beauty rather than by violence I said  how much  better how much happier to do that which  will make mankind love you then create a  world all about YOU of hate and fear one  year later following america's entry  into world war two and at nearly 70  years old oakley continued this personal  and artistic mission by joining with the  Citizens Committee of the Army and Navy  while originally founded to provide  leisure materials and entertainment to  boost morale the committee adopted a new  initiative and fall 1941 to produce  portable trip tix for three paneled  altarpieces for use on American  battleships military bases and airfields  around the world and by war's end the  committee would send 460 trip tix by 70  painters and sculptors to five  continents with oakley painting 25 here  I show postcards on the right there of  two later wartime trip tix each of which  has its own story that i'll briefly  touch on later oakley was one of the  first artists to collaborate on the  triptych campaign but her interest in  the triptych format began long before  the war indeed oakley had an abiding  fascination with Medieval and  Renaissance art and literature filling  her home even by 1914 with curiosities  from her European travels like this 14th  century Italian triptych and while her  early murals often drew on this three  paneled format her 1924 design of an  ultra piece for the alumni room or Lum  niehaus scuze me at Vassar College will  be her first standalone triptych and  would have a lasting influence on her  artistic efforts in fact oakley  described her design for her second more  time triptych christ stealing the storm  scene below here as being directly in  the vein of that basser altarpiece  intriguingly in oakleys citizens  community collaborations the artists  pacifism did not prompt an outright  objection to the war effort praised at  the end of her career for capturing the  triumph of love over hatred and law and  order over a fallen humanity oakley  instead used her wartime works to  portray men resurrected relationships  restored conflicts great conflicts  resolved in a world redeemed and this  too is the message of the angel  victory in this battle against evil that  was the second world war good would  inevitably prevail studies for the  altarpiece indicate that Oakley  initially chose the Apostle Paul's words  at the end of Romans eight as her  inscription comparing the American  troops to embolden conquerors in a  spiritual army and their military  campaigns abroad to an epic  eschatological battle quote against the  spiritual forces of evil in the heavenly  realms and this text anticipates  rhetoric of American leaders like  general dwight d eisenhower who declared  before d-day in 1944 that his troops  were embarking on a Great Crusade so the  triptych participates in a broader  contemporary American tendency to  elevate the nation's cause to Universal  even biblical proportions to see war as  Paul Addison writes quote as a myth  which enshrined Americans and Britons  essential purity a parable of good and  evil unquote many other artists took up  the epic themes of warrior saints and  angels to a triptych for the attack  transport ship USS Haywood shows st.  George as an ancestral Christian soldier  watching over a marching infantry  battalion and in an altarpiece for the  fort benning Airborne School and nearly  armored angel looks more like a heavenly  paratrooper staring with his American  compatriot at falling parachutes beneath  the bright white cross two of oakleys  sketches for a nun executed wartime  altarpiece entitled the whole armor  contains similar ironclad figures which  recalled Michael and st. George from her  angel of victory rather than specific  biblical or mythological characters  these are purely symbolic clad with the  breastplate of righteousness the shield  of faith the helmet of salvation as the  accompanying scription states but in  both cases oakleys armored angels and  saints quite literally embody the  greater almost spiritual cause of  American troops which one New York Times  author described in this way our men  have been brought up in ways of worship  which call themselves by different names  they're Catholics Protestants Jew  but religion by whatever name is a part  of the American Way of life the way of  life which these men once peaceful  citizens have become fighting men to  preserve so the faith with which these  painted figures were girded was the  faith that American troops were deployed  to defend despite the emphasis in her  initial inscription on the certainty of  victory for as Paul writes in Romans 8  neither death nor life nor things  present nor things to come nor anything  else in all creation can overcome the  power of God oakley eventually abandoned  the passage perhaps due to its  militaristic conquest oriented tone  instead she selected inscriptions that  describe a heavenly victory that had  already been achieved and on humanity's  behalf at left she paired the texts on  Michaels defeat of the Great Dragon in  Revelation 12 with an image of that arc  angel as he sheathed his sword and  stands atop the vanquished beast and at  center the word surrounding Gabriel  announced those glad tidings that  Christ's birth has brought quote peace  on earth goodwill toward men while also  including Christ's command the Peter  upon his arrest by the Roman soldiers to  put up thy sword with the battle won in  all conflicts east oakleys triptych is  an apocalyptic vision that predicts an  end to global strife in an ideal world  of universal community at the climax of  history so it's perhaps unsurprising  that oakleys oakley first expressed this  religious neo Hegelian perspective in  response to the first world war two  canvases the woman clothed with the Sun  in the old serpent cast out convey  oakleys desire for global reconciliation  and unity amidst international conflict  originally exhibited at the  architectural league of New York in 1916  the compositions would later form part  of her 1924 altarpiece the great wonder  a vision of the Apocalypse from vassar  in the old serpent Oakley surrounded the  scene with the very texts that would  later encircle st. George an angel  victory and similarly perched the  Archangel Michael atop that serpent or  Satan as he cast it out of  7 meanwhile the woman clothed with the  Sun captures the story from revelation  of the manchild who was to rule all  nations as the text describes and who  escapes the devouring jaws of the dragon  Aziz lifted up to a celestial throne  this apocalyptic scene insists that  despite the chaos of this present age  humanity will come together at the end  of time and find peace under a single  heavenly government in these canvases  which constitute her first reaction to  the great war oakley responded to the  troubling unstable political climate  with the very same subject she would  later employ at the onset of World War  two and yet as Oakley embarked on her  first war time triptych in 1941 one  wonders why she selected such veiled  references to an eschatological age of  peace and unity why not employ more  explicitly pacifist or ardently anti-war  imagery like the incarcerated  insurrectionists christ of george  bellows blessed are the peacemakers are  the condemning assemblage with  instruments of human cruelty in Claude  bucks the things that are Caesar's  perhaps Oakley realized that the main  significance of her altarpieces was not  their personal political import but the  service that the Citizens Committee  hoped the triptych smite provide as one  publication states the trip tix were to  carry comfort and strength to this  generation in its overwhelming task of  defending the present and preserving the  future I think that's a line that we've  heard a lot so far in this conference  that kind of language and so though rare  within American Protestantism and  perhaps even contrary to its mode of  worship where in Christ incorporeal word  rather than his physical body is what  provides access to God wartime trip tix  nevertheless developed a unique  Protestant purpose Sally Prommy and  David Morgan have suggested that these  wartime altarpieces provided visual  technology for the transaction between  divine and human realms and this is  quite true so the members of the  Citizens Committee describe how trip tix  uniquely help Chaplin's to hold unsaid  the connection between man and God and  likewise chaplains like Jerome Sullivan  of the USS Pennsylvania wrote of how the  under their charge had quote felt the  long arm of the Lord outstretched to  save and protect them on numerous  occasions unquote and so the seagoing  subject matter of the painting like  Oakley's christ walking on the water a  version of which was displayed aboard  the USS Pennsylvania not only  communicated divine truths to the troops  but also served as a sort of patron  saint able to intercede on their behalf  so it's unsurprising for instance that  oak leaves Madonna the sea was welded to  the bulkhead in the mess hall of the USS  leeds town and became a permanent  fixture of that ship the specificity of  a triptych subject for its intended  military audience hence became a  spiritual imperative and what served  Oakley well in this regard was her  admiration of Italian poet Dante  Alighieri particularly his talent for  fusing eternal themes with contemporary  subjects since the angel of victory was  intended for Brooklyn's Floyd Bennett  airfield which was a naval air station  defending New York City during the war  Oakley sketches show her efforts to  include particular eyes renderings of  American airmen to accompany her angelic  central figures yet oakley also searched  for biblical and historical models that  merge the mission of these Airmen with  that of their heavenly counterparts so  as we've seen the warrior Michael could  serve as a timeless emblem for fighter  pilots in their airborne warfare and  small details like the link the central  figures to the Aviators like the gold  designs on Gabriel's heavenly vestments  which which echoed the Airman's  parachute harnesses or the dub of the  holy spirit above Gabriel's head which  parallels the naval aviator badge below  him tying scenes to their military  audience made these divine figures more  accessible more receptive to their  entreaties and hence more efficient  tools in what prominent Morgan call this  economy of ritualized exchange in which  the sacred image is performed but these  images were not solely a means of  ritualized transaction there are also  outward signs of God's presence and  promises  in places where divinity otherwise  seemed most absent constructed from  lightweight materials trip tix allowed  troops to erect a makeshift church  anywhere a photo of oakleys Christ  stealing the storm beneath the gun  turrets of the USS in Massachusetts  attests and one citizens committee  member also noted that quote triptych  scan be set up in a jiffy and whether  it's in a steaming jungle or on the  rolling deck of a fighting ship or in  the back of a truck under the fire of  guns there is created an altar a church  chaplains and soldiers describe the  transformative even transported quality  of trip tix recounting how the church ly  appearance of the altarpiece produced a  dignified spiritual atmosphere which  overcame the clamour and ugliness of war  and as the founder of the citizens  committees triptych campaign Louise  Morgan once said at the camp's there  seemed to be no beauty anywhere at  stations were there no chapels we supply  little movable altars and trip tix and  there you have a nice little church  before you letters also poured into the  Citizens Committee headquarters praising  the trip tix atmospheric effects one  anonymous soldier wrote of an oakley  triptych on the eve of battle you can  undoubtedly imagine what a crews  mess-hall looks like and sounds like  with steam pipes hissing fans and  blowers rumbling someone on the deck  above hammering and the smell of food  cooking however amid all this we held  our divine services this morning but  there was one essential item a triptych  which when placed atop a mess table  draped with a cloth took all the  inconveniences to worship away and made  the hall almost the Cathedral these  facial effects recall belden lanes  remarks about sacred fate space as very  often an ordinary place ritually set  apart to become extraordinary for just  as in photographs like these where the  men gather below three towering turrets  to sit pause and gaze inwardly in  silence on this stern deck normally a  wash and frenzied action in the booming  of guns wherever an altar was there too  was a church moreover just as  triptych sin the Middle Ages were said  to fold inward and thereby hold their  sacred images and violet modern  altarpieces came to be described as  spiritual armor that could shield  soldiers Souls against the dehumanizing  nature of combat and photographs of  religious services in the field one  commentator wrote we find two seemingly  incongruous objects of the ancient past  quote the media Boyle shape of the  helmet worn by the United Nations men  and the triptych which appear among such  strange new shapes as those of the robot  bomb the bazooka the Jeep the bulldozer  the tank the airplane the submarine in  fact one US Army Brigadier General  described quote a colorful and  impressive outdoor ceremony to dedicate  an infant inventory division artillery  units new altar piece of st. Barbara the  patron saint of artillery men he wrote  the mast colors of the artillery  organizations of division flanked the  work which was mounted on two of our  howitzers the inspiring ceremony will  long be remembered by those present and  while army trip tix were painted on  plywood panels navy ultra pieces were  made of bulletproof steel this was due  to the fact of course that ships needed  to throw any flammable materials  overboard before sailing into battle and  hence any triptych had to be non  flammable but the very bulletproof  materiality of naval trip dicks  heightens these shielding associations  juxtaposed with the defenses of modern  warfare altarpieces quite literally  developed this armor like protective  status as the Citizens Committee  insisted the triptych in all its beauty  was as necessary to man's soul as the  implements of war are necessary for the  soldier with American troops battling to  preserve Faith Family and freedom a task  in which the triptych as a secret  bulwark was indispensable artists  certainly understood the production of  ultra pieces to be a patriotic and a  spiritual duty of course since the war  essentially halted the sale of most  artworks trip tix were also one primary  and popular  means of artistic expression at the time  because most finished trip tix went  straight to their wartime destinations  before they could be shown in public  studies and drawings like these on the  screen allowed artists to continue  displaying their work before an American  civilian audience Oakley exhibited  preparatory studies at venues like the  Metropolitan Museum of Art the Corcoran  the Pennsylvania Academy still artists  received only token wages for each  triptych just two hundred dollars for  materials and roughly four weeks of work  and elderly philadelphia-based Oakley  was also somewhat of an outlier in this  triptych movement most artists worked in  New York and were much younger or lesser  known by putting aside her more  lucrative illustration and portrait  projects and instead be richly producing  and exhibiting trip tix Oakley proved  that they were central to both her  personal artistic vision and her  professional aspirations perhaps she  also found the military a more suitable  audience for her religious symbolism  since even by 1940 as Patricia lycos  claims her esoteric imagery had very  little appeal for a broader public  intrigued by surrealism and abstraction  and disillusioned by two world wars in a  depression to conclude Oakley also  understood the moral and spiritual  imperative of the triptych campaign and  she was rewarded for this commitment to  it many also pieces were dedicated in  impressive formal military ceremonies  where artists like Oakley who's seen in  the center of the photograph were  praised for their artistic contributions  to the war effort the desire to equate  an artist's work with military service  recalls strategies of war posters from  both world wars like rivets our bayonets  drive them home which compared the tasks  of factory workers at home to the fight  abroad and sometimes quite violent  fashion yet the connections between  altarpieces and their makers in world  war two were more complex altarpiece is  embodied not only divine messages but  also the artists themselves in this key  moment in American history when the  aspirations of artists and the anxieties  of the nation were expressed in  universalizing biblical terms  these trip tix gave artists a uniquely  symbolic means of joining the war as  surrogates sent into battle on their  creators behalf one New York Times  author wrote this many of the altar  pieces will come back as battle scarred  as any veteran or lie with the good ship  that bore it at the bottom of the sea  but all will have served the cause that  is the cause of all of us thank you

